    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Can you get more specific though? Like, are there certain theories that link specific environments—for example, high-latitude vs tropical regions—to specific cognitive traits or temperamental archetypes? I'm curious if there's any consensus on how things like ancestral social structures or survival demands might have actually resulted in measurable differences in population-level traits.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Some theories suggest that the harsh, resource-scarce environments of high latitudes might have selected for traits emphasizing extreme cooperation and endurance, while warmer, more resource-rich tropical areas could have favored different social dynamics. Survival demands in different climates likely shaped distinct cognitive strategies, such as different approaches to risk assessment or social complexity. These environmental pressures would have filtered which genetic predispositions were most successful for group survival in those specific ancestral settings.",
      "source": "target",
